Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.190
Film Courage: Ken, I have a quote here from the late
author Philip Roth and quote is the road

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:11.429
to hell is paved with works in progress. Dr. Ken Atchity, Author/Producer:
Yes, that's worse than good intentions.

00:00:11.429 --> 00:00:18.000
What's that a question like
it's a statement and a question I guess

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.689
in that do you have any ideas on that I
know it's it's kind of a thing I'm just

00:00:21.689 --> 00:00:26.130
throwing out here but well I think
unfinished works are that the kind of

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:31.619
characteristic debris of the writers
life of any visionaries life I mean that

00:00:31.619 --> 00:00:38.010
the discards that Leonardo da Vinci had
in his studio were prodigious and you

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:43.500
you just have a lot of ideas and you
can't do them all and one of the methods

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:50.910
that I've developed over the years is to
actually set aside a new idea and give

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:56.010
it a two-week rest and and check in with
it in two weeks to see whether you even

00:00:56.010 --> 00:01:01.289
remember the idea and and the key to
doing that as a writer is not to write

00:01:01.289 --> 00:01:07.920
it down see it's like a basic rule that
I have is that if you have an idea and

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.150
as a writer your immediate goal is to
write it down get it down because that's

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:16.320
what writers do if you can train your
brain not to do that you're gonna have

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.490
much better product in the long run
because you're not writing every single

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:26.189
thing down including every bad thing so
by not writing it down when you revisit

00:01:26.189 --> 00:01:32.520
it two weeks from now if you don't
remember it that's great that means one

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:37.860
lousy idea went away as opposed to
trying to do something with every single

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:43.710
idea that you have and so I think that's
part of what creative people learn is

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:49.850
how to manage their own minds and
because I was an accountant son I

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:55.770
long-ago analyze the creative process
the creative mind and decided that they

00:01:55.770 --> 00:02:00.750
weren't just crazy the way a lot of
people think they actually is a method

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:06.000
to creativity and in fact it spreads
across every discipline whether you're a

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.830
physicist or a mathematician or you know
an inventor or a writer or an artist

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:19.680
creative process has the same steps in
the same general pattern so if you can

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.450
understand the process then you aren't
nearly as neurotic as you are if you

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:30.890
don't understand it you know one of my
goals was to not be crazy

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:35.760
Salvador Salvador Dali said one of my
favorite things about this he said the

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:42.060
difference between myself and a madman
is that I am not mad and I love that

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.290
because it's it's exactly what I'm
talking about it's it's understanding

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:50.160
the method in your madness as
Shakespeare put it and if you can

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.020
understand that then you don't have to
be unhappy and neurotic in order to be a

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:59.490
productive writer
speaking of which writing things down do

