of writers over my career I think that
you the ones who are unhappy and looking

00:19:10.929 --> 00:19:15.700
for attention you really get tired of
them fast if you're dealing with them

00:19:15.700 --> 00:19:19.390
all the time I mean if they're in your
family and everything and you only have

00:19:19.390 --> 00:19:23.470
to see them once a week okay fine you
only have to see them at Thanksgiving

00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:28.659
dinner okay even better but if you're
you know somebody who deals with them

00:19:28.659 --> 00:19:33.159
every day sooner or later the ones who
are constantly complaining go on to your

00:19:33.159 --> 00:19:39.280
life is to a shortlist and those are not
the ones you're looking for and they're

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:45.220
what I call pseudo artists who ends up
end up not being productive most of the

00:19:45.220 --> 00:19:49.450
time you know who but who are longing to
be artists but don't have the mental

00:19:49.450 --> 00:19:55.059
discipline to to actually do it going
back to Philip Roth again seeing

00:19:55.059 --> 00:19:59.140
interviews with him toward the later
years of his life he had moved from

00:19:59.140 --> 00:20:04.360
New York City - sort of the Connecticut
woods to be left alone and all of the

00:20:04.360 --> 00:20:08.920
journalists said isn't it lonely here
for you and he said it is but I enjoy it

00:20:08.920 --> 00:20:13.330
there's no there's no friction there's
nothing because guess he guess it was

00:20:13.330 --> 00:20:18.490
after port noise complaint or one of the
he was just receiving so much attention

00:20:18.490 --> 00:20:22.930
and he was bombarded with people's
opinions and this was just an easier way

00:20:22.930 --> 00:20:27.130
for him to continue and I know this is a
common thing of sort of taking yourself

00:20:27.130 --> 00:20:31.630
off the map so that you can create but
yet the loneliness was worth it

00:20:31.630 --> 00:20:36.520
versus the friction yeah and obviously
it worked for him because other people

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:40.840
who would go off to live in the woods
end up not being productive because they

00:20:40.840 --> 00:20:45.190
they think that's gonna solve their
problem I mean I learned this the hard

00:20:45.190 --> 00:20:50.170
way because I had to finish a book early
in my academic career and I decided I'd

00:20:50.170 --> 00:20:55.150
go to my parents late college and just
sit there and finish it and of course I

00:20:55.150 --> 00:20:59.830
almost got nothing done that summer
because one thing led to the other

00:20:59.830 --> 00:21:04.420
people would stop by to visit because
there was the lake and you know the lawn

00:21:04.420 --> 00:21:09.670
would need attending or the cabin itself
needed fixing and I used every excuse I

00:21:09.670 --> 00:21:14.440
could possibly think of to avoid sitting
down to write and this is where I worked

00:21:14.440 --> 00:21:20.440
out a lot of the the theories that are
in my view of creativity is that summer

00:21:20.440 --> 00:21:26.650
because pressure is what causes
creativity to work best lack of pressure

00:21:26.650 --> 00:21:31.960
actually works against creativity so as
a producer I'd much rather have a

00:21:31.960 --> 00:21:36.970
low-budget film to deal with where every
single thing that you do has to be a

00:21:36.970 --> 00:21:41.410
solution to the fact that you don't have
enough money to do it so it becomes more

00:21:41.410 --> 00:21:45.640
creative and you tell the crew that you
know we have to have creative solutions

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:49.930
to these issues because money is not
going to solve this we don't have the

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:53.740
money
and of course studio films don't have

00:21:53.740 --> 00:21:58.570
that issue they have endless pockets and
so on but nonetheless you can see that

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:03.010
if there was more discipline to them a
lot of them would be better than they

00:22:03.010 --> 00:22:08.110
are when you see it a film that has six
or seven writers listed you know at the

00:22:08.110 --> 00:22:12.220
beginning as screenwriters you know that
this was just caused by

00:22:12.220 --> 00:22:16.509
you know they they didn't work with
writer number three long enough they

00:22:16.509 --> 00:22:21.220
just fired him and brought in rider
number four and that was the expensive

00:22:21.220 --> 00:22:26.710
way to do it and but there's there's a
challenge in the pressure that comes and

00:22:26.710 --> 00:22:32.470
time pressure is the number one pressure
more than financial even that works on

00:22:32.470 --> 00:22:37.960
behalf of creativity if you only have a
limited amount of time I always found

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:43.629
that I did my most creative work half an
hour before committee meeting because I

00:22:43.629 --> 00:22:48.039
hated committee meetings and I still
find that when I have to go to something

00:22:48.039 --> 00:22:54.159
that I'm not wild about going to I have
suddenly extremely creative an hour

00:22:54.159 --> 00:23:02.289
before that and rather than resenting
that I my my creativity around that so

00:23:02.289 --> 00:23:07.779
that that's when I do it whenever I can
and I think that that's what we have to

00:23:07.779 --> 00:23:11.529
learn about our minds is how to how to
kind of trick them into behaving the way

00:23:11.529 --> 00:23:15.460
we want them to behave you know to
producing what we want them to produce

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:20.230
so you talked about the type C
personality and then in your book how to

00:23:20.230 --> 00:23:24.250
escape lifetime security and pursue your
impossible dream a guy to transforming

00:23:24.250 --> 00:23:29.379
your career is it chapter six a day in
the life of type C and I was wondering

00:23:29.379 --> 00:23:34.509
if I could talk about that how is that
Dana life is it a structured day is it

00:23:34.509 --> 00:23:40.419
well it's different from you know it's
going to be different for every type C

00:23:40.419 --> 00:23:46.720
and it's going to be different from from
people who are not type C's and how it's

00:23:46.720 --> 00:23:53.980
different is that the type C's has
learned how to arrange his day to fit

00:23:53.980 --> 00:23:59.289
his type to fit his mind to fit his or
her mind

00:23:59.289 --> 00:24:06.490
some people are night owls and some
people are you know early birds and the

00:24:06.490 --> 00:24:11.860
early bird writer is not going to write
late at night because she not

00:24:11.860 --> 00:24:15.730
comfortable writing away at night she's
comfortable in the morning so if she

00:24:15.730 --> 00:24:21.399
gets up at four o'clock she's gonna give
herself as much time as she has

00:24:21.399 --> 00:24:25.870
attention span for to do her writing in
the morning which is when

00:24:25.870 --> 00:24:30.430
I love to do mine because no one
interrupts you from Florida to 7:00 in

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:36.460
the morning but if you're a night owl as
toking was he wrote Lord of the Rings

00:24:36.460 --> 00:24:41.500
completely after one o'clock at night
because he he was so busy all the time

00:24:41.500 --> 00:24:46.059
before then and had a family and
everything else so he wrote in the

00:24:46.059 --> 00:24:50.620
middle of the night and sometimes he
wrote all night and and just went to you

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:56.170
know went off to school to teach without
any sleep at all but that was okay

00:24:56.170 --> 00:25:00.160
because he was doing what he loved so
his his day would be arranged

00:25:00.160 --> 00:25:04.690
differently than you know the day of
someone who was on a clock that's not

00:25:04.690 --> 00:25:09.100
their clock somebody wish to show up for
a nine o'clock job it's not on their own

00:25:09.100 --> 00:25:13.750
clock and their day is going to be
probably one that they're upset with

00:25:13.750 --> 00:25:19.000
most of the time whereas if you're you
know if you're type C and you in charge

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:23.080
of your own life you're gonna rearrange
it around the patterns that work best

00:25:23.080 --> 00:25:29.380
for your mind and that I think that's a
crucial part of becoming a Type C is

00:25:29.380 --> 00:25:35.160
having your own kind of day okay I go to
a lot I go to meetings to sell the

00:25:35.160 --> 00:25:41.350
properties that we've developed and I
don't like going to meetings because it

00:25:41.350 --> 00:25:45.970
takes a lot of time to get there and
once you're there there's a certain

00:25:45.970 --> 00:25:50.440
amount of wasted time and then you do it
the thing it's always fun you know even

00:25:50.440 --> 00:25:54.309
though you dreaded it so I try to
arrange my day so that I'm doing

00:25:54.309 --> 00:25:59.559
something that is very productive like I
wish I didn't get any work done and my

00:25:59.559 --> 00:26:02.440
wife is telling me what are you talking
about you went to three pitch meetings

00:26:02.440 --> 00:26:06.400
that's it you know two at three
different networks yeah I know but I

00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:11.290
don't feel like I got any work done I
mean that's that's just your mental view

00:26:11.290 --> 00:26:17.800
of things and so I think that everyone's
type there every type C day is going to

00:26:17.800 --> 00:26:21.910
be different and what you really need to
do is if you're interested in pursuing

00:26:21.910 --> 00:26:27.910
this for yourself as you need to figure
out what is your ideal day I mean is it

00:26:27.910 --> 00:26:31.780
important to you to go for a walk is it
important to you to meditate is it

00:26:31.780 --> 00:26:37.720
important to you to spend next time on
your creative work and is it import do

00:26:37.720 --> 00:26:39.710
you just
next time with your family and all of

00:26:39.710 --> 00:26:44.200
those things and you sit there and
rearrange your date to make that work

00:26:44.200 --> 00:26:50.150
that's what time management is all about
and how do you do it you know no matter

00:26:50.150 --> 00:26:53.660
how busy you are
there are busier people I was reading

00:26:53.660 --> 00:26:57.500
Michelle Obama's book and nobody could
be busier than the president in the

00:26:57.500 --> 00:27:01.460
United States and the first lady of the
United States but somehow they they made

00:27:01.460 --> 00:27:06.350
time for everything they needed to make
time for which tells you that there was

00:27:06.350 --> 00:27:11.990
time management at work because
certainly if anybody had infinite things

00:27:11.990 --> 00:27:17.420
to do an infinite work to do it would be
those two but if they can do it you can

00:27:17.420 --> 00:27:23.240
do it too I think philip k dick loved to
write at night and he would stay up all

00:27:23.240 --> 00:27:27.050
night and i'm not sure if some of it was
maybe chemically chemically induced but

00:27:27.050 --> 00:27:32.230
then when he married another wife she
wanted him to write from nine to five

00:27:32.230 --> 00:27:37.790
she said i'm very middle-class who's why
I like these hours and so he eventually

00:27:37.790 --> 00:27:41.420
got his own apartment which he called
the hovel and it was dirty and he felt

00:27:41.420 --> 00:27:46.370
that he did his best writing when when
he wanted to in this you know sort of

00:27:46.370 --> 00:27:50.060
dirty apartment and it just lent to what
he was doing so it's just interesting

00:27:50.060 --> 00:27:55.610
how yeah you know we're the Hubble
syndrome is is interesting because I

00:27:55.610 --> 00:28:01.010
think every creative person can relate
to that President Obama called his the

00:28:01.010 --> 00:28:06.500
whole and it was always a room that had
to be found in any house they were in

00:28:06.500 --> 00:28:10.790
where nothing could be touched you know
he could do whatever he wanted and

00:28:10.790 --> 00:28:14.000
usually their papers all over the floor
and everything like and it was there

00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:18.380
that he finished a book or a speech and
so on and the Hubble is the same idea

00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:22.580
and I noticed that you know I've always
been the same way by the weekend my

00:28:22.580 --> 00:28:27.560
office is a complete mess there are
things all over the floor and and then

00:28:27.560 --> 00:28:33.050
by Monday it's all shipshape and when
you think about that it's nothing but

00:28:33.050 --> 00:28:37.370
the externalization of the creative
process because the creative process is

00:28:37.370 --> 00:28:42.560
making order out of chaos you know in
st. John's Gospel says in the beginning

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:47.990
was the word and the Word was with God
and the Word was God he was the

00:28:47.990 --> 00:28:50.880
beginning with God all things were made
through him

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:56.700
you know and he goes on and talks about
the light let there be light etc so when

00:28:56.700 --> 00:29:01.320
when the artist creates something he is
taking a bunch of little things and

00:29:01.320 --> 00:29:06.120
creating order out of them and so the
externalized version of that is is

00:29:06.120 --> 00:29:10.620
living in a messy place and
straightening it up when as much as you

00:29:10.620 --> 00:29:15.570
have to whenever you have to and if
there's some external force that is

00:29:15.570 --> 00:29:20.850
forcing you to straighten it up then
that creative person is not in charge of

00:29:20.850 --> 00:29:26.760
their own life and they can be you can
always find a way to do it there's a

00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:33.030
touching short story by Doris Lessing
called to room 19 I think that's the

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:37.920
name of it - room 16 maybe in any case
it's it's one of her greatest short

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:42.750
stories and it's about a housewife who
longed all of her life to have a room of

00:29:42.750 --> 00:29:48.870
her own and and it was because she
couldn't she couldn't be herself in her

00:29:48.870 --> 00:29:52.860
family and she couldn't do what she
wanted to do and she didn't feel free

00:29:52.860 --> 00:29:59.960
and I won't tell you how it ends because
it's not a fun ending but it's a very

00:29:59.960 --> 00:30:05.220
tragic example of what happens if you
don't take charge of your own creative

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:12.450
life interestingly enough Tolkien wrote
a very introspective piece called leaf

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:19.770
by niggle strange title but niggle was
the name of a painter who had this

00:30:19.770 --> 00:30:26.850
amazing vision of a spectacular forest
and his vision was so clear that he

00:30:26.850 --> 00:30:31.230
could see every tree in the forest
clearly every animal in the forest every

00:30:31.230 --> 00:30:38.160
leaf on every tree in the forest and
because he was so busy he never got

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:43.140
around to painting more than a single
leaf that's the way the story ends up

00:30:43.140 --> 00:30:49.290
you know ends up and it's really
Tolkien's agonized argument for why he

00:30:49.290 --> 00:30:52.980
had to write in the middle of the night
because he determined that he was not

00:30:52.980 --> 00:30:58.200
gonna be niggle you know even though he
wrote something like 40 books on

00:30:58.200 --> 00:31:03.150
linguistics and different languages and
of course Lord of the Ring

00:31:03.150 --> 00:31:08.820
and the silmarillion and many other
great works he felt that he had barely

00:31:08.820 --> 00:31:15.450
gotten to one tree in his forest and
only that because he rode all night so

00:31:15.450 --> 00:31:21.030
that that is a terrible thing to kind of
carry around is the the belief that you

00:31:21.030 --> 00:31:26.850
can do amazing things but you don't have
time to do them and the answer is that's

00:31:26.850 --> 00:31:31.050
not right you do have time I mean where
did Michelangelo find his time where did

00:31:31.050 --> 00:31:36.570
Lonard eventually find his time you know
they all had the exact same number of

00:31:36.570 --> 00:31:42.030
hours that we have and your job is to
take your vision seriously and find

00:31:42.030 --> 00:31:46.770
those hours to make it happen or someone
like Alice Monroe who when she first

00:31:46.770 --> 00:31:50.700
started out was I guess raising four
children and she didn't want the other

00:31:50.700 --> 00:31:54.420
housewives in the neighborhood to know
that she was a writer because she

00:31:54.420 --> 00:31:58.290
thought she would get the weird label
which she ended up getting and she

00:31:58.290 --> 00:32:03.150
didn't care anyway but um I guess when
you you win a Nobel Prize it doesn't it

00:32:03.150 --> 00:32:08.550
takes all that away but yeah but she
would do it when the children were

00:32:08.550 --> 00:32:11.400
napping and if the other housewives
knocked on the door you know she would

00:32:11.400 --> 00:32:16.320
put it all away she didn't want people
to know but so I realized that stigma is

00:32:16.320 --> 00:32:21.360
probably no longer today no it's it's
fill there is it yeah it's it's it's a

00:32:21.360 --> 00:32:26.940
it originates in people's families and
and it's it's when you you you announced

00:32:26.940 --> 00:32:30.059
to your father or your mother that
you're going to be a writer or you're

00:32:30.059 --> 00:32:33.150
going to be a circus clown or you're
going to be a dancer

00:32:33.150 --> 00:32:39.300
or you're going to be an actress and
that is where it starts because they you

00:32:39.300 --> 00:32:42.210
know the normal response is what are you
gonna do for a living

00:32:42.210 --> 00:32:49.040
and that haunt you there's another book
one of my books my talk about learning

00:32:49.040 --> 00:32:53.420
as you go into the creative life
learning who your true friends are and

00:32:53.420 --> 00:32:58.170
learning who your friendly associates
are because you lose most of your

00:32:58.170 --> 00:33:02.940
friendly associates when you make a
decision to go from a irrational life to

00:33:02.940 --> 00:33:07.950
a creative life and once they that class
I gave a regular class at UCLA that was

00:33:07.950 --> 00:33:12.870
called keeping your spirits up for
creative people and one time there were

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:16.160
a bunch of actress
in the class and I said at the beginning

00:33:16.160 --> 00:33:19.990
of the class I said let's go around the
circle and everyone introduce themselves

00:33:19.990 --> 00:33:27.290
and tell me tell me your name and where
you're from and what is the worst

00:33:27.290 --> 00:33:32.840
question that you could be asked at a
bar or a cocktail party in LA and and

00:33:32.840 --> 00:33:38.750
have you respond to it and when when
lady said you know she was from Arkansas

00:33:38.750 --> 00:33:45.800
and her name was Joe and the worst
question that she had in LA was when he

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:50.210
gonna go back to Arkansas and work in
the post office again and I said howdy

00:33:50.210 --> 00:33:54.290
we answered that that's terrible
and she goes usually by bursting into

00:33:54.290 --> 00:33:59.870
tears and leaving the room and I said
well hopefully this class will find some

00:33:59.870 --> 00:34:05.750
help for that the next woman said her
name was Jenny and she was from

00:34:05.750 --> 00:34:11.480
California and she said and the worst
question I have is what have you been in

00:34:11.480 --> 00:34:17.030
big lately that I've seen and I said
yeah that terrible question - she goes

00:34:17.030 --> 00:34:21.770
and I understood what is your answer she
goes the Pacific Ocean and I always

00:34:21.770 --> 00:34:25.960
loved that because it showed that here's
a creative person who has figured out

00:34:25.960 --> 00:34:30.710
how to protect her mind from the
inevitable things that are going to

00:34:30.710 --> 00:34:35.720
happen in the big world people are not
born with sensitivity they don't walk

00:34:35.720 --> 00:34:38.780
out of our homes on the way to a party
going I'm going to be particularly

00:34:38.780 --> 00:34:43.129
sensitive today and the first thing they
say to an actress they meet us what have

00:34:43.129 --> 00:34:46.820
you been in bigs that I've seen it's not
because they're mean or that they're

00:34:46.820 --> 00:34:50.990
nasty people but maybe they are but it's
probably because they aren't being

00:34:50.990 --> 00:34:56.270
sensitive and you having that answer
instantly bonds you with them and makes

00:34:56.270 --> 00:35:00.710
them respect you for respecting yourself
enough to not take their questions

00:35:00.710 --> 00:35:05.090
seriously you don't ever have to answer
any question that somebody gives you

00:35:05.090 --> 00:35:09.620
unless you feel like it so when she
answers it that way

00:35:09.620 --> 00:35:14.630
she disarms the whole situation whereas
the first girl is not doing such a good

00:35:14.630 --> 00:35:18.320
job because she shouldn't be going to
parties until she can answer that

00:35:18.320 --> 00:35:23.330
question about going back to Arkansas
and working for the post office and

00:35:23.330 --> 00:35:27.710
that's a another example of protecting
your mind and not protect

00:35:27.710 --> 00:35:34.040
in your mind having the introspection to
know how to deal and you were talking

00:35:34.040 --> 00:35:39.290
about like whether people react how do
people react to you're deciding to be

00:35:39.290 --> 00:35:43.430
creative you know I always say it's it's
like there's this guy down the street

00:35:43.430 --> 00:35:48.830
who's been painting in his garage for
the last ten years and you know when the

00:35:48.830 --> 00:35:52.580
neighbors are talking they're talking
about a mess he's crazy you know he's a

00:35:52.580 --> 00:35:54.950
crackpot
he's been doing that for twenty years

00:35:54.950 --> 00:35:59.030
whatever and then one day they read in
the paper that one of his paintings sold

00:35:59.030 --> 00:36:04.130
for a million dollars and what did they
what do they say I always knew the guy

00:36:04.130 --> 00:36:07.040
was a genius
you know he had to be a genius to be

00:36:07.040 --> 00:36:12.170
working that hard but everything
suddenly changes when the world accepts

00:36:12.170 --> 00:36:17.830
your creativity but the only way you're
gonna get to that point is if you

00:36:17.830 --> 00:36:22.280
absolutely control what you're doing and
believe in it yourself

00:36:22.280 --> 00:36:28.730
and even if you don't believe in it keep
acting as though you do and in words you

00:36:28.730 --> 00:36:32.450
don't have to believe in things you
don't have to feel good in order to work

00:36:32.450 --> 00:36:37.540
and you don't have to feel good in order
to do good work you can work and

00:36:37.540 --> 00:36:42.950
normally when you work you get rid of
these feelings anyway so this is all

00:36:42.950 --> 00:36:49.910
examples of dealing with the creative
mind and how to get it to be your friend

00:36:49.910 --> 00:36:54.470
as opposed to be something you're scared
of and don't want to take off to a cabin

00:36:54.470 --> 00:37:00.260
in the woods well I noticed with the
star is born' which is now up for an

00:37:00.260 --> 00:37:06.380
Oscar we're just about a month away or
so that what struck me about the film

00:37:06.380 --> 00:37:13.280
was the loneliness of the creative
process and the lack of people around

00:37:13.280 --> 00:37:18.800
them when they were working on things
and whether it was his drinking or

